# CLI Surface Attribute

This document describes the `[CliSurface]` attribute used to mark classes as CLI command surfaces.

**Location:** `Attributes/CliSurfaceAttribute.cs`

```csharp
namespace Cyrena.CLI.Attributes;

[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class, Inherited = false, AllowMultiple = false)]
public sealed class CliSurfaceAttribute : Attribute
{
    public string? Description { get; set; }
}
```

## Purpose

The `CliSurfaceAttribute` marks a class as a CLI command surface, indicating it contains CLI command methods. Only classes marked with this attribute will be discovered and registered by the `CliService` when scanning assemblies.

This prevents accidental registration of non-CLI classes and provides explicit opt-in for CLI functionality.

## Usage

### Basic Usage

```csharp
using Cyrena.CLI.Attributes;

[CliSurface]
public class ConfigurationCommands
{
    [CliCommand("set")]
    public void SetConfig(
        [CliParam("key")] string key,
        [CliParam("value")] string value)
    {
        // Command implementation
    }
}
```

### With Description

```csharp
[CliSurface(Description = "Application configuration commands")]
public class ConfigurationCommands
{
    // Commands...
}
```

## Discovery Behavior

When `CliService.RegisterCommandsFromAssembly()` is called, it scans for classes that meet ALL of these criteria:

1. Is a class (`IsClass == true`)
2. Is not abstract (`IsAbstract == false`)
3. Is marked with `[CliSurface]` attribute

```csharp
// In CliService.RegisterCommandsFromAssembly()
var types = assembly.GetTypes()
    .Where(t => t.IsClass && !t.IsAbstract && t.GetCustomAttribute<CliSurfaceAttribute>() != null);
```

## Examples

### Example 1: Simple Command Surface

```csharp
[CliSurface]
public class HealthCommands
{
    [CliCommand("check")]
    public void CheckHealth()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Health check passed!");
    }
}
```

### Example 2: Multiple Command Surfaces in One Assembly

```csharp
[CliSurface(Description = "Database operations")]
public class DatabaseCommands
{
    [CliCommand("migrate")]
    public async Task Migrate() { }
}

[CliSurface(Description = "User management")]
public class UserCommands
{
    [CliCommand("create")]
    public void CreateUser([CliParam("name")] string name) { }
}
```

### Example 3: Class Without CliSurface (NOT Registered)

```csharp
// This class will NOT be discovered by CliService
public class RegularService
{
    [CliCommand("won't-work")]  // Ignored - class not marked with [CliSurface]
    public void SomeMethod() { }
}
```

### Example 4: Abstract Class (NOT Registered)

```csharp
// Abstract classes are ignored even with [CliSurface]
[CliSurface]
public abstract class BaseCommands
{
    // Won't be registered
}

// But concrete implementations work
[CliSurface]
public class ConcreteCommands : BaseCommands
{
    [CliCommand("run")]
    public void Run() { }
}
```

## Best Practices

1. **Mark all CLI command classes** - Always use `[CliSurface]` on classes containing CLI commands.

2. **Add descriptions** - Use the `Description` property for documentation:
   ```csharp
   [CliSurface(Description = "Handles application lifecycle commands")]
   ```

3. **Keep surfaces focused** - Group related commands in the same surface class:
   ```csharp
   [CliSurface]
   public class ConfigCommands { }  // All config-related commands

   [CliSurface]
   public class DataCommands { }    // All data-related commands
   ```

4. **Don't mix concerns** - Avoid putting CLI commands in service classes that have other responsibilities.

## Registration

### Automatic Discovery

```csharp
var service = new CliService();
service.RegisterCommandsFromAssembly(typeof(Program).Assembly);
// Only [CliSurface] classes will be registered
```

### Manual Registration

```csharp
var service = new CliService();
service.RegisterCommandsFromType<ConfigurationCommands>();
// Will throw if ConfigurationCommands is not marked with [CliSurface]
```

**Note:** `RegisterCommandsFromType` validates that the type has `[CliSurface]` and throws `ArgumentException` if missing.

## Error Handling

If a class without `[CliSurface]` is manually registered:

```csharp
service.RegisterCommandsFromType<RegularService>();
// Throws: ArgumentException: Type 'RegularService' must be marked with [CliSurface] attribute.
```
